1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,403
How to tackle case PORTFLOW.

2
00:00:02,403 --> 00:00:07,022
A step-by-step method you can repeat on any case like this one.

3
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:21,004
Cases like this follow the operations & engineering improvement model: Baseline KPIs, then bottleneck/root cause, then optimize/simulate, then pilot, then ROI + KPIs.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:31,897
Start with one question: How does PortFlow reach <= 85% yard utilization and < 45-minute truck turns at +20% volume, within $10M?

5
00:00:31,897 --> 00:00:42,331
Split it into three branches with no overlap: Placement (where boxes sit), Order and timing (when they leave), Information and movement.

6
00:00:42,331 --> 00:00:45,400
Each person on the team owns one branch.

7
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:51,245
Within twenty minutes, write down a hypothesis you can prove wrong.

8
00:00:51,245 --> 00:00:54,282
Here: PortFlow's yard is static and blind.

9
00:00:54,282 --> 00:01:09,613
Demand-based zoning (MIP + CRAFT), dynamic slotting on one tracked database, and shared release status cut handling time and dwell enough to meet both objectives without new land; AI AGVs are piloted, not bet on.

10
00:01:09,613 --> 00:01:12,433
If the data kills it, pivot and say so.

11
00:01:12,433 --> 00:01:14,024
Judges reward honesty.

12
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:17,831
Use only the tools that fit.

13
00:01:17,831 --> 00:01:21,948
MIP: container-group-to-block assignment.

14
00:01:21,948 --> 00:01:26,366
CRAFT: pairwise exchange layout improvement.

15
00:01:26,366 --> 00:01:29,980
Demand forecasting with seasonality.

16
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:36,205
Then do the quick math: Today: 21,120 / 24,000 TEU = 88% full.

17
00:01:36,205 --> 00:01:41,828
Year 5 at today's stay: about 25,300 TEU, over capacity.

18
00:01:42,830 --> 00:01:51,347
Score all three options with explicit weights: target attainment, return, payback, downside resilience and budget headroom.

19
00:01:51,347 --> 00:01:55,502
The budget is a hard gate: an option over budget cannot win.

20
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:58,808
Then build a solution ladder.

21
00:01:58,808 --> 00:02:00,797
Level one, the quick win.

22
00:02:00,797 --> 00:02:05,015
Level two, the cheapest option that meets the target.

23
00:02:05,015 --> 00:02:07,641
Level three, the value maximiser.

24
00:02:07,641 --> 00:02:09,551
Level four, the stretch.

25
00:02:09,551 --> 00:02:14,644
Your recommendation should explain where it sits on this ladder.

26
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:18,736
Finally, protect your time.

27
00:02:18,736 --> 00:02:21,831
0:00 to 0:25, Read & align.

28
00:02:21,831 --> 00:02:24,698
0:25 to 0:55, Quick math.

29
00:02:24,698 --> 00:02:28,367
0:55 to 1:40, Package & finance.

30
00:02:28,367 --> 00:02:31,806
1:40 to 2:30, Deck (8 slides).

31
00:02:31,806 --> 00:02:35,131
2:30 to 3:00, Rehearse & Q&A.

32
00:02:35,131 --> 00:02:40,864
Video three shows the answer this method produces.
